1. Arithmetic Operators

Used for mathematical calculations.

+ (Addition) → a + b
- (Subtraction) → a - b
* (Multiplication) → a * b
/ (Division) → a / b
% (Modulus) → a % b (remainder)
** (Exponent) → a ** b (power)
// (Floor Division) → a // b (rounds down)


2. Comparison (Relational) Operators

Used to compare values (result is True or False).

== (Equal to)
!= (Not equal to)
> (Greater than)
< (Less than)
>= (Greater than or equal to)
<= (Less than or equal to)
3. Logical Operators

Used to combine conditional statements.

and → True if both conditions are true
or → True if at least one condition is true
not → Reverses the result


4. Assignment Operators

Used to assign values to variables.

= → x = 5
+= → x += 3 (same as x = x + 3)
-= → x -= 3
*= → x *= 3
/= → x /= 3
%= → x %= 3
//= → x //= 3
**= → x **= 3



5. Identity Operators

Used to compare memory locations.

is → True if both variables refer to the same object
is not → True if they don’t
6. Membership Operators

Used to test if a value exists in a sequence.

in → True if value is present
not in → True if value is not present
7. Bitwise Operators

Work on binary (bits) of numbers.

& (AND)
| (OR)
^ (XOR)
~ (NOT)
<< (Left shift)
>> (Right shift)